.SH "NAME"
nvme-vendor.d \- the defaults of storage vendors for their NVMe\-oF controllers
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.sp
.nf
\fI/usr/local/etc/nvme/vendor\&.d/*\&.conf\fR
\fI/run/nvme/vendor\&.d/*\&.conf\fR
\fI/usr/local/lib/nvme/vendor\&.d/*\&.conf\fR
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
Storage vendors name defaults for their controllers: the I/O policy of the subsystem, and the ctrl_loss_tmo of a controller\&. libnvme applies them to a controller right after it connected it, so every connect through libnvme gets them: \fInvme connect\fR, \fInvme connect\-all\fR, the connections of the NBFT, and those of nvme\-discoverd\&. A controller that reconnects after a loss keeps its values; one that is connected anew gets them again\&.
.sp
There is one INI file per vendor, with a [Model] section for each set of values, e\&.g\&. 50\-netapp\&.conf:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
[Model]
model = NetApp ONTAP Controller
iopolicy = queue\-depth
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
[Model]
model = NetApp ONTAP Controller
transport = tcp
ctrl\-loss\-tmo = \-1
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.SH "MATCH"
.sp
\fImodel\fR matches the controller\(cqs model as sysfs shows it, \fItransport\fR each a shell glob (\fI*\fR, \fI?\fR, \fI[\&...]\fR), against the value without its trailing whitespace\&. So \fImodel = NetApp*\fR or \fItransport = tcp|rdma\fR work as in a rule, and the udev rules 71\-nvmf\-<vendor>\&.rules translate one to one\&.
.SH "VALUES"
.PP
\fIiopolicy\fR
.RS 4
The I/O policy of the subsystem, if it is an NVM subsystem (as the
.RE
.PP
\fIctrl\-loss\-tmo\fR
.RS 4
The ctrl_loss_tmo of the controller, in seconds, or \-1 for no limit\&. Not for the loop transport\&.
.RE
.SH "ORDER"
.sp
For a controller, the first of these that sets a value wins:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' 1.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP "  1." 4.2
.\}
the command line, e\&.g\&.
\fInvme connect \-\-ctrl\-loss\-tmo\fR;
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' 2.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP "  2." 4.2
.\}
the connection\(cqs
\fIctrl\-loss\-tmo\fR
in nvme\-fabrics\&.conf(5);
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' 3.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP "  3." 4.2
.\}
the files of vendor\&.d;
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' 4.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP "  4." 4.2
.\}
libnvme\(cqs default (a ctrl_loss_tmo of 600, the kernel\(cqs iopolicy)\&.
.RE
.sp
libnvme takes a ctrl_loss_tmo of the connection other than the default (600) as given by the user, so a connection that asks for exactly 600 gets the vendor\(cqs value\&.
.sp
The files of vendor\&.d follow the rules of udev rules and sysctl\&.d: the directories are /usr/local/etc/nvme/vendor\&.d, /run/nvme/vendor\&.d and /usr/local/lib/nvme/vendor\&.d\&. A file in a directory before replaces the file of the same name in a directory after it, and a link to /dev/null (or an empty file) turns it off\&. All files are then read in the order of their names, whatever their directory, and where several sections match a controller, the later one wins\&. The shipped files are named 50\-*\&.conf, so an administrator\(cqs 90\-local\&.conf overrides them, and a file named like a shipped one replaces it\&.
.SH "FILES"
.PP
/usr/local/etc/nvme/vendor\&.d/*\&.conf
.RS 4
The administrator\(cqs files\&.
.RE
.PP
/run/nvme/vendor\&.d/*\&.conf
.RS 4
Files for the running system only\&.
.RE
.PP
/usr/local/lib/nvme/vendor\&.d/*\&.conf
.RS 4
The defaults nvme\-cli ships, with the values of the vendors\*(Aq udev rules\&.
.RE
.SH "THE UDEV RULES"
.sp
The rules 71\-nvmf\-<vendor>\&.rules set the same values after the kernel added a controller, also on one whose connection asked for another ctrl_loss_tmo\&. While nvme\-discoverd runs, its service masks them in /run/udev/rules\&.d, so that a connection\(cqs own ctrl_loss_tmo stays\&.
